      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2016-01-28T06:17:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-01-28T06:17:33Z",
    "body": "I think we have decided not to change this. To summarize the points made here:\n1. Large dates do have use cases, even if you find those use cases unusual\n2. It is easy to enforce such restrictions yourself, via the `max` attribute.\n3. The spec has no basis for picking a cutoff; you suggest 9999, but there is no clear reason why that is better than e.g. 9998 or 9000 or 2100 or (current year). It is an application-specific decision.\n4. Accidentally choosing such far-future dates are not a problem for UAs that present calendar widgets for the input; it would have to be very deliberate on the user's part.\n5. For UAs that do not present such widgets (if they exist), this proposal may prevent typos such as 20015, but does not prevent most typos, such as 2105 or 0215, so the proposal does not address the actual problem that is trying to be solved. (That is, presuming the problem to be solved is to get years in approximately the 1900-2100 timeframe---but see point 3.)\n\nRegardless, thanks for taking the time to explain your position to us and coming to work with the spec on something you think is a problem with the platform. Please feel free to continue to bring such issues to our attention!\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
